.TH RAND 3
.SH NAME
rand, srand \- random number generator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.B void srand(unsigned seed);
.PP
.B int rand(void);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rand
uses a multiplicative congruential
random number generator
to return successive pseudo-random
numbers in the range from 0 to 2\u\s715\s0\d\-1.
.PP
The generator is reinitialized by calling
.I srand
with 1 as argument.
It can be set to a random starting point by calling
.I srand
with whatever you like as argument.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's page gave the period as 2**32, which was its state's width and not a
property of the recurrence.  See below.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/rand.c
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B The state wraps at 2**48, and the sequence is still v7's, value for value.
That is worth knowing before anyone tries to "fix" the modulus.
.PP
v7 held the state in a signed
.B long
and relied on it wrapping at 2**32.  A
.B long
is one word here \- 41 bits signed, 48 unsigned \- so the state is declared
.I unsigned
and wraps at 2**48, where the arithmetic is defined rather than merely customary.
.PP
The generator is unharmed by the wider modulus: the multiplier is odd and
congruent to 1 mod 4 and the increment is odd, which is what makes the period the
full modulus, whatever power of two that is.
.PP
And the values do not change.
.B The low bits of a truncating product depend only on the low bits of its
.BR operands ,
so x mod 2**32 evolves here exactly as it did on a 32-bit machine, whatever the
state's high bits are doing \- and
.I rand
returns bits 16..30, which are inside that window.  A wider state cannot change
what is handed back.  Only an
.I srand
above 2**32 could, and no v7 program does one.
